WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.510
A hallucination is a sensation without a stimulus.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:07.439
But why do hallucinations occur?

00:00:07.439 --> 00:00:10.610
To answer that we have to look at perception itself.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:13.210
We think of perception as a passive process--

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.740
an input arrives and is received.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.580
But actually, perception is more like a guess

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:20.429
of what is out there in our environment.

00:00:20.429 --> 00:00:23.779
It’s the result of sensory input, sure,

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:25.599
but also another component that may be

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:30.069
just as important: Our prior beliefs, or expectations.

00:00:30.069 --> 00:00:32.419
Some scientists think that hallucinations occur

00:00:32.419 --> 00:00:35.120
when strong beliefs create an imbalance

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.030
with sensory input.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:39.230
To see if they could witness this overpowering

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:42.410
of external input by beliefs researchers created

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.350
a game that caused auditory hallucinations

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.879
and observed the effects on brains and behavior.

00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:52.059
Their experiment is a twist on Pavlovian training.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.140
Whereas Pavlov’s dog learned to associate

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.200
food with sound--Instead, the scientists trained

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.120
human players to associate sight with sound.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.040
In the game, a checkerboard flashes on

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.600
and a tone is played simultaneously.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.080
With enough repetitions, participants start to expect

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.360
to hear the sound when they see the image,

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.640
even when the sound isn’t actually played.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:19.320
They were played the tone at different intensities

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.030
and sometimes no tone at all.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:23.030
As the game went on,

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.950
there were more and more no-tone events.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:27.869
The participants were asked to press a button

00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:29.490
each time they heard the tone.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.140
They were also told to press longer

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.640
based on their confidence in their judgment.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.200
When players were presented a checkerboard

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.310
without the tone but still pressed the button,

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.640
that was considered a hallucination.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.640
Four different groups were selected to play:

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:47.980
A control group that didn’t have

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.540
a history of hallucinations or psychosis

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.460
Daily hallucinators with no diagnosed psychotic illness.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:57.509
Non-hallucinators with psychotic illness.

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:01.570
And finally, daily hallucinators with a psychotic illness.

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.299
Crunching the data, researchers found that

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:05.950
all participants experienced conditioned hallucinations

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.049
during the game.

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:08.700
The people who already heard voices were

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.729
the most susceptible to the effect

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:12.360
and were the most confident they heard tones

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.610
when none were actually played.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.450
Brain scans conducted during the game showed

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:17.720
what regions were activated

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.260
when conditioned hallucinations occurred.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.860
Researchers then devised a model for hallucination

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:28.900
that provided different weights for beliefs and inputs.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.010
Fitting the behavioral data into the model,

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:34.230
they found that hallucinators do overweight beliefs

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.030
and this correlates with regions of the brain

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.680
that we know relate to perceptual beliefs.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.480
Those with psychosis were less able

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:41.930
to update their beliefs,

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:44.640
which corresponded with activity in the hippocampus

00:02:44.659 --> 00:02:47.340
and cerebellum, which typically guide our memories

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.450
and our sensory and motor predictions

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.700
about our bodies in the world.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.270
The model suggests that when players w/out psychosis

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.681
were trained in the game, they formed a strong belief

00:02:57.681 --> 00:03:00.519
that the sound would coincide with the checkerboard

00:03:00.519 --> 00:03:02.549
but as the game progressed, they caught on

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:05.090
that more no tone trials were being played,

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:08.510
because they were able to update their beliefs.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.750
The players with psychosis, on the other hand,

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:14.320
had trouble adapting as the game changed.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.440
The nexts steps are to use larger sample sizes

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.879
and machine learning tactics to further distinguish

00:03:21.879 --> 00:03:26.099
between voice-hearers with and without psychosis.

00:03:26.099 --> 00:03:28.230
The hope is that researchers could diagnose

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.739
if a hallucinator is on a path to psychosis

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.640
and suggest more targeted treatments in the future.

